{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 20, :comments ["<b>Feeds.</b> Like one who feeds on ash — it harms and does not benefit. Or its meaning is like 'roams after wind' (Hosea 12:2)." "<b>Deceiver.</b> An epithet, for epithetical nouns change, on the pattern of ורגל מועדת (Proverbs 25:19)." "<b>Heart.</b> The fool." "<b>Misleads him.</b> And this word is pejorative throughout Scripture, for it means turning aside from the straight path. And the word להטות (Exodus 23:2) I have explained."], :book :isaiah, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 44, :notes {1 "The phrase ורגל מועדת is hyper-literally rendered; Ibn Ezra invokes the Proverbs verse to illustrate that epithets can take varying forms."}, :source-witnesses [["Ibn Ezra on Isaiah, by M. Friedlander; Society of Hebrew Literature, London 1877" "https://www.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH001338443"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:58:01.219545998Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}